# Python
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Scratch strategy sources written by calibration runs. Regenerated
# deterministically from a committed generator and a recorded seed, so
# there is nothing here a review needs and tens of thousands of files
# a review does not.
runs/tmp-*/

# transient: the proposing agent type-checks here, then deletes it
src/strategies/_proposal_*.py
CLAUDE_TASK.md

# the agentic loop writes per-candidate working state here
loop/candidates/

# Raw per-run dumps from the calibration measurements. 431 MB for Task 57 alone
# (arm-b-runs.json is 281 MB), and one `git add .` puts that in history forever.
# They regenerate deterministically from the committed script and the seeds it
# names; the committed `*.json` summary keeps every per-campaign verdict a
# reader needs. Summaries stay tracked -- only the raw dumps are ignored.
#
# Task 59's `_sources` (3,000 files, 12 MB) was committed before this rule and
# stays tracked -- git keeps tracking a file it already knows, ignore rules only
# govern what is untracked. It is left alone rather than removed: rewriting
# history to save 12 MB is not a trade worth making.
docs/reviews/*/arm-*-runs.json
docs/reviews/*/_sources/

# The champion's weights, swapped in by `nq_campaign.py --stage validate` the
# way `current.py`'s contents are, and deleted in the same `finally`. Ignored so
# a crashed validate leaves an untracked file rather than a staged one. The
# durable copy lives in the campaign's `weights/` directory, addressed by digest.
runs/current.weights

# build-time only: the PDF reader for scripts/fetch_fulltext.py.
# Deliberately NOT in .venv -- environment_fingerprint hashes every importable
# distribution, and the engine never calls a PDF reader.
.venv-tools/

# **Third-party paper full texts -- never commit these.** `fetch_fulltext.py`
# downloads the body of a paper so the screen can read more than an abstract,
# and 315 of them (20 MB) were tracked until 2026-10-17. They are other
# people's copyrighted work: the registry records `license: not recorded` for
# every one of the 400 sampled, so we cannot show a right to redistribute any
# of them, and an MIT licence on this repository would assert exactly that.
#
# They stay on disk and the pipeline still reads them -- `screen_papers.py`
# screens on a body when one is present, and the tests need the files, not the
# tracking. Anyone cloning this re-fetches with `scripts/fetch_fulltext.py`,
# which is the honest arrangement: we hold what we were allowed to download,
# and we redistribute none of it.
data/papers/text/
